"**Shakespearean plays that explicitly name or allude to pears (fruit)**  \n\n| Play | Allusion / quote | Where to find it |\n|------|------------------|------------------|\n| **All\u2019s\u202fWell\u202fThat\u202fEnds\u202fWell** | \u201cYour virginity \u2026 is like one of our French withered pears.\u201d | Act\u202fI,\u202fScene\u202f1 (lines\u202f158\u201170).\u30102\u2020L1-L5\u3011 |\n| **Romeo and\u202fJuliet** | \u201cAn open\u202fet\u202fcaetera, thou a poperin\u202fpear!\u201d (Mercutio\u2019s comic line). | Act\u202fII,\u202fScene\u202f1 (line\u202f840).\u30108\u2020L1-L7\u3011 |\n| **The Winter\u2019s\u202fTale** | \u201cI must have saffron to colour the Warden\u202fpies.\u201d (referencing the hard, winter\u2011storing \u201cwarden\u201d pear). | Act\u202fIV,\u202fScene\u202f5 (stage note\u2014see the page cited in the BBC article about the warden pear).\u30107\u2020L0-L6\u3011 |\n| **Merry\u202fWives\u202fof\u202fWindsor** | \u201cas crest\u2011fallen as a dried pear.\u201d | Act\u202fIV,\u202fScene\u202f5 (dialogue).\u30108\u2020L11-L14\u3011 |\n\nThese are the only Shakespeare plays that contain a fruit\u2011specific reference to a pear (or a closely related pear variety). No other of Shakespeare\u2019s plays have a penciled reference to \u201cpear\u201d in the same sense.\n\n*Sources*\n\n1. OpenSourceShakespeare page for *All\u2019s\u202fWell\u202fThat\u202fEnds\u202fWell* \u2013 quoted passage.  \n2. OpenSourceShakespeare page for *Romeo &\u202fJuliet* \u2013 Mercutio\u2019s \u201cpoperin pear\u201d.  \n3. BBC article \u201cOld Warden pear saved by Shuttleworth College students\u201d \u2013 the line about \u201cwarden pies\u201d.  \n4. OpenSourceShakespeare page for *Merry\u202fWives\u202fof\u202fWindsor* \u2013 the \u201cdried pear\u201d comparison.  \n\n(If you require exact line numbers or further context, let me know.)"